<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucius Postumius Albinus (consul 234 BCE)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/216-bce">216 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucius Postumius Albinus was a Roman general, consul in 234 and 229 BCE. In 216 BCE, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a>, he was sent against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> of the Po valley, but the Boii ambushed and destroyed his army in the Litana forest. He was killed while consul-elect for 215 BCE, and the Boii are said to have made his skull into a gilded cup.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lucius Postumius Albinus belonged to the patrician gens Postumia and was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulus-postumius-albinus">Aulus Postumius Albinus</a>, consul in 242 BCE. Most of what is known of his career and death comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>'s Ab Urbe Condita. In his first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a>, in 234 BCE, he campaigned against the Ligures, and it has been conjectured that he was praetor in the following year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In his second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a>, in 229 BCE, he and his colleague Gnaeus Fulvius Centumalus fought the Illyrian queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teuta">Teuta</a>. Albinus commanded the land forces, captured Apollonia, relieved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Epidamnos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issa-dalmatia">Issa</a>, forcing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a> to give up both sieges, and subdued several local tribes. His command was extended in 228 BCE so that he could conclude the peace, after which he sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legatus">legates</a> to the Aetolian and Achaean Leagues to explain the war and its terms. Unlike his colleague, he was not granted a triumph.</p><p>He then disappears from the record for a decade. In 216 BCE, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> under way and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> short of experienced commanders, he was elected praetor for a second time without being present in the city, and given the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Gallia Cisalpina</a>. With two legions and reinforcements he marched against the Boii, who had risen and declared for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>. While on campaign he was elected in absentia as consul for the following year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Crossing the Litana forest, his army was ambushed and mostly destroyed at the Battle of Silva Litana; the survivors tried to escape over a bridge and were cut down by Boii guarding it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> writes that the Boii took his head to their most sacred temple and covered the skull with beaten gold for libations. The news so alarmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> that shops closed until the Senate had the aediles order them open. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-claudius-marcellus">Marcus Claudius Marcellus</a>, elected in his place, abdicated when the augurs declared the election invalid, and Quintus Fabius Maximus Verrucosus became consul.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lucius Postumius Albinus (consul 234 BC)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 218 BCE they attacked Rome's new colonies at Placentia and Cremona and supported Hannibal; in 216 BCE they ambushed and killed the consul-elect <b>Postumius</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/boii-400">Boii</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-226" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lucius Postumius Albinus (consul 234 BCE)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-226">What was here in 226 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
